 "n_seeds": 20,
 "finding": "The loop replay would need is wired, and it is far too weak to matter. MBON11 is the only engram carrier whose Kenyon-cell targets are enriched for the trained ensemble, 1.86 times, and every other carrier that fires offline contacts the ensemble LESS than chance, so on anatomy alone it is the only candidate. But one of its spikes delivers 0.228 mV to one of its targets, so even firing flat out at 50 Hz it would supply 0.057 mV, which is 0.82% of the 7 mV threshold gap. Silencing it completely therefore cannot move its targets by anything a Kenyon cell would notice, however often it fires. That ceiling is anatomy and the model's own constants, not a measurement that might come out differently. The one seed of 20 in which the carrier fires at all looks at first like the loop working, the trained ensemble speeding up by +7.6%. It is not. In the same run the Kenyon cells the carrier does NOT touch speed up by +7.9% and the whole population by +7.7%, a difference of 0.29 percentage points. That is a global shift in the network's state, which is what a small perturbation usually produces at this operating point, and not disinhibition of the trained ensemble. An earlier version of this script reported that shift as the loop working; the contacted-versus-untouched split is here because it was not. In the 19 seeds where the carrier is silent the ensemble moves +0.02%, p = 0.68. The conclusion is about the design and not about this run. An engram held downstream of the cells whose reactivation is being measured reaches them through a return leg three orders of magnitude too weak, so Kenyon-cell ensemble replay is not a question this model can answer positively, and a positive would have to be explained by something other than the memory.",
